          body: "Pressure in a spa arrives in clusters - two sickness calls, a VIP arrival and a broken room heater land in the same half hour. Managers who thrive under this are not calmer by nature; they run a consistent triage order.\n\nFirst, guest safety and legal duty - anything touching health, safety or hygiene outranks everything else, always.\n\nSecond, guest-facing impact in the next hour - the arriving VIP couple and the 10:00 bookings come before anything a guest cannot yet see.\n\nThird, revenue protection - can the sickness gaps be covered by moving treatments between rooms and therapists before you start cancelling? A cancelled £180 ritual is gone forever; a 30-minute delay handled with a genuine apology and a gesture usually is not.\n\nFourth, team strain - who is now doing more than planned, and what do they need to sustain it?\n\nA second lens helps with bigger decisions: is this reversible? Reversible decisions (move a treatment, adjust a rota, trial an offer) should be made fast and adjusted later. Irreversible ones (dismissing someone, cancelling a VIP, committing to a refund policy) deserve the extra hour of thought. Most new managers get this backwards - agonising over reversible calls and rushing the irreversible ones.",
