          body: "The most reliable feedback structure in management practice is situation-behaviour-impact (SBI), and it fits on the back of a hand.\n\nSituation: name the specific moment - 'this morning, with the 10 o'clock facial guest'. Behaviour: describe what you observed, as a camera would record it - 'the consultation lasted under two minutes and skin concerns weren't asked about'. Impact: state the consequence - 'she mentioned at checkout that the treatment didn't address her congestion, and she declined products she'd normally be an obvious match for'.\n\nThen stop talking. Ask for their view. Half the time you will learn something operational - the previous treatment ran over, so the consultation was squeezed - which turns a performance conversation into a scheduling fix.\n\nWhat makes SBI work is what it leaves out: verdicts on character. 'You're careless' invites a defence of the whole self; 'the consultation was two minutes' invites a fix. The same structure carries praise, and praise delivered with this precision ('the way you handled the overheated guest in the thermal suite - moving her to the relaxation room and bringing iced water before she asked - is exactly the standard') teaches the team what excellence looks like far faster than 'well done today'.\n\nTiming matters as much as structure: coach within a day, privately, in minutes. An observation saved up for a quarterly review has usually expired.",
